<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: #ffffff; padding: 20px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px; margin-bottom: 20px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0; font-size: 2em;">Lesson Planner – Theme # I'm at the Market – Lesson # 1</h1>
    <p style="margin: 10px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 1</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Listening</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 3rd Grade (CEFR: A1.1)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Places I Can Go</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> I'm at the Market.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Listening: Receptive: Demonstrate comprehension of simple spoken instructions. Interactive: Identify the main points in a conversation.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Listening: Can demonstrate comprehension and perform tasks based on oral instructions related to identifying location (e.g., "Point to the house."). Can identify the main points of a conversation about specific places or locations (e.g., recognizing “market” in a short chant or sentence). Can recognize words and phrases about places from one peer to another (e.g., "I'm at school." "Where is she?" "She's at school.").</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 25px;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher starts the class by playing a short, repetitive "Places" song (e.g., "Where are you going today? To the market, to the market!"). While playing the song, the teacher shows large, colorful flashcards of "market," "school," "home," and "park." Teacher points to each flashcard as the word is heard in the song or spoken clearly, encouraging students to listen and point with her. This activates prior knowledge of common places and introduces new vocabulary receptively.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher holds up the flashcard for "market," clearly enunciating "market" several times, emphasizing the /m/ sound. She then says "I'm at the market" while gesturing as if walking there. She repeats this with "school," emphasizing the /sk/ sound, and says "I'm at school." The teacher models the contraction "I'm" and the preposition "at." She also models polite expressions by saying "Excuse me, where is the market?" or "Thank you!" when shown a place.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding by asking simple, concrete questions.
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Showing 'market' flashcard) "Is this a house?" (No) "Is this a market?" (Yes)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Showing 'school' flashcard) "Do we eat here?" (Maybe, at cafeteria) "Do we study here?" (Yes) "Is this a school?" (Yes)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Pointing to self) "I'm at the market. Am I at home?" (No)</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5-7 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Places I Can Go".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short audio recording (or teacher-read dialogue with character voices) of a conversation between two children, Maya and Leo, discussing where they are or where they want to go. The audio incorporates the target vocabulary (market, school, home, park) and grammar ("I'm at...", "Are you at...?").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Dialogue Example:</b><br>
                    <i>Maya: "Hi Leo! I'm at the market. It's busy here."</i><br>
                    <i>Leo: "Oh, hi Maya! I'm at school. Are you at the market?"</i><br>
                    <i>Maya: "Yes, I'm at the market. I want to buy fruit. Where is Clara?"</i><br>
                    <i>Leo: "She's at the park. She's playing."</i><br>
                    <i>Maya: "Okay! Thank you, Leo!"</i><br>
                    <i>Leo: "You're welcome! Bye!"</i>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Listening: Can identify the main points of a short conversation about specific places or locations (e.g., recognizing “market” in a short chant or sentence).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist Question:</b> Teacher plays the audio once. "What are Maya and Leo talking about? Places or food?" (Places)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False:</b> Teacher plays the audio again, pausing after each key sentence. Students listen and say "True" or "False."
                            <ul>
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Maya is at the market." (True)</li>
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Leo is at home." (False, he's at school)</li>
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Clara is at the park." (True)</li>
                            </ul>
                        </li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10-12 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Listening.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Listening comprehension of simple spoken instructions about locations. Reinforcing Contractions ("I'm"), Prepositions of place ("at the market"), and target vocabulary (market, school, home, park, library, store, hospital).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice – Listen and Circle:</b> The teacher distributes a worksheet with pictures of different places (market, school, home, park, library, store). The teacher says sentences using "I'm at the..." for each place. Students listen and circle the picture corresponding to what they hear. Example: "I'm at the market." (Students circle the market picture). "I'm at the library." (Students circle the library picture).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled Practice – Listen and Point (TPR):</b> The teacher places large flashcards of places around the classroom. The teacher gives instructions like "Point to the school!" or "Walk to the market!" Students listen and physically point or move towards the correct flashcard. The teacher gradually increases the speed or adds new places from the vocabulary list (e.g., "hospital," "bakery," "restaurant").</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Interaction – "Where Am I?" Game (Receptive Listening):</b> Students work in pairs. Each pair has a set of small flashcards or pictures of places. One student (Student A) closes their eyes or turns away. The other student (Student B) picks a card and says, "I'm at the [place on card]." Student A listens and points to the correct picture from their own set, or points to a larger class flashcard. They then swap roles. Example: Student B says, "I'm at the park." Student A points to the park picture.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">15 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Places I Can Go".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> "Listen and Act Out" – A guided, collaborative role-play focused on following and understanding simple oral instructions and statements about locations.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher divides the class into small groups of 3-4 students. Each group receives a small set of place flashcards (e.g., market, home, school, park).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher reads out a short scenario or sequence of instructions. Students in each group listen carefully and choose the correct flashcard, then perform a simple action.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Example sequences:
                            <ul>
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Listen: I'm at the market. What do you do at the market? (Students point to 'market' card and pretend to buy fruit.)"</li>
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Listen: She's at school. What does she do at school? (Students point to 'school' card and pretend to read a book.)"</li>
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Listen: He's at the park. What does he do at the park? (Students point to 'park' card and pretend to play.)"</li>
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Listen: I'm at home. I'm quiet. (Students point to 'home' card and pretend to sleep.)"</li>
                            </ul>
                        </li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher observes how well students follow the instructions and connect the action to the place, encouraging all students to participate receptively.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Focus:</b> Fluency in listening comprehension, collaborative interaction within groups, and demonstrating understanding through non-verbal actions.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">8-10 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during the "Listen and Act Out" activity and an individual "Exit Ticket."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students will listen to a final set of simple instructions from the teacher.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Exit Ticket:</b> Each student receives a small paper with blank spaces. The teacher says: "Draw the market." "Draw school." "Draw home." Students listen and draw the specified place.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Listening Comprehension:</b> Ability to correctly identify and react to spoken place names and simple instructions.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Target Vocabulary:</b> Recognition of core words like "market," "school," "home," "park."</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Clarity of Response:</b> Correct pointing/drawing/acting for each instruction.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> On the back of their drawing paper, students are asked to silently think about:
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What new place did I hear today?"</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Was it easy or difficult to understand the teacher?" (Students can draw a happy/sad face).</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h4>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is one place you remember from today's lesson?"</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is something you can hear and point to now?"</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in this listening-focused lesson? The use of TPR and flashcards seemed very effective for aural comprehension. Students responded well to the "Listen and Point" activities.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or scaffolds are needed for the next lesson? Some students still confused "market" and "store." I need to ensure clearer differentiation and more repetition of these specific words. Perhaps a small picture for each new word on the students' desks for constant reference.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> In Lesson 2 (Reading Comprehension), students will read short sentences and dialogues that use the same vocabulary ("market," "school," "home," "park," "library," "store") and grammar ("I'm at...") introduced today, reinforcing visual recognition of the words they have just heard.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">2-3 min</td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>